// A pending async_yield() left over at shutdown must be SETTLED, not just
// released.
//
// The shape below is the ordinary one, and it is a refcount cycle:
//
//   promise p = async_yield();   // the frame local is the surviving ref
//   await p;
//
// A parked coroutine does not hold a ref on the promise it awaits, so the
// last reference to p lives inside the coroutine's own saved frame, and the
// coroutine is reachable only from p's reaction list. Dropping the driver's
// reference at teardown therefore never reaches zero: the coroutine, its
// frame svalues and both promises survive to exit. Settling instead breaks
// the cycle, because a reaction enqueued after shutdown has begun is freed
// synchronously.
//
// The leak is only observable when the driver exits with the yield still
// queued, which is what a single-file run does -- it shuts down before the
// loop turns. Run this file on its own under a sanitizer build to see it:
//
//   ../build-asan/src/driver etc/config.test \
//       -ftest:single/tests/efuns/async_yield_shutdown.lpc
//
// Unfixed that reports "328 byte(s) leaked in 6 allocation(s)", every block
// Indirect with no Direct root -- LeakSanitizer's signature for a pure
// cycle. In a full run the yield settles normally long before shutdown and
// this file simply passes.

nosave int parked;

async void parker() {
  promise p = async_yield();

  parked = 1;
  await p;
  parked = 2;
}

void do_tests() {
  // Touch the filesystem so the run exercises a valid_read apply: a
  // single-file run that triggers none of them trips the testsuite's own
  // "MASTER valid inherit functions are not being called!" check, which
  // would make the manual run above look like a failure of this file.
  ASSERT(stringp(read_file("/single/master.lpc", 1, 1)));

  parker();
  ASSERT2(parked == 1, "the body should run up to its await and park there");
}
